00:07:04.721 --> 00:07:06.812
What changes from the interview version.

89
00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:08.436
Here is a quick table:

90
00:07:08.716 --> 00:07:13.111
Interview, and Production (Spring Boot 4). longdw + PriorityQueue:

91
00:07:13.391 --> 00:07:32.271
Redis hash (5 x 2,000 counters) + sorted set, shared by all consumers. one stream forever: per-hour windows with TTL; last hour = current window (or sum the last 2 for sliding). separate increment and heap update: one Lua script: no lost updates between instances.

92
00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:41.493
Measured: max overcount 3 against the bound e or w, N = 28, and the top 5 matched the exact counts.

93
00:07:41.773 --> 00:07:50.007
At very high event rates, pre-aggregate in the consumer (count locally for 1 s, then one HINCRBY per item) to cut Redis calls.

94
00:07:51.357 --> 00:07:52.232
Part 8.

95
00:07:52.512 --> 00:07:53.621
Common mistakes.

96
00:07:54.871 --> 00:07:56.663
Using the sketch for rare items.

97
00:07:56.943 --> 00:08:00.997
The relative error is large when the true count is small compared with N.

98
00:08:01.277 --> 00:08:02.365
Never decaying.

99
00:08:02.645 --> 00:08:09.685
Trending means recent, so use time-bucketed sketches (one per minute, summed over a window) or exponential decay.

100
00:08:09.965 --> 00:08:14.637
Hashing with correlated functions across rows, which defeats the min-of-rows trick.

101
00:08:15.987 --> 00:08:16.883
Part 9.

102
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:18.507
Follow-up questions.

103
00:08:19.757 --> 00:08:20.631
Question.

104
00:08:20.911 --> 00:08:24.517
How would you compute trending over the last hour across many servers?

105
00:08:24.797 --> 00:08:26.162
Thirty-second answer:

106
00:08:26.442 --> 00:08:28.383
Each server keeps per-minute sketches.

107
00:08:28.663 --> 00:08:37.773
Sketches are mergeable (cell-wise add), so a reducer sums the last 60 minutes from all servers, then picks the top-K from the merged candidates.

108
00:08:38.053 --> 00:08:40.741
This is a classic Flink or Kafka Streams job.

109
00:08:42.091 --> 00:08:42.965
Question.

110
00:08:43.245 --> 00:08:46.061
Count-Min versus Space-Saving or Misra-Gries?

111
00:08:46.341 --> 00:08:47.707
Thirty-second answer:

112
00:08:47.987 --> 00:08:56.093
Space-Saving keeps exactly k counters and directly gives heavy hitters with a guaranteed error, but it isn't as easily mergeable.

113
00:08:56.373 --> 00:09:00.256
Count-Min answers point queries for any item and merges trivially.

